<li id="cite&#95;note-50"><span class="mw-cite-backlink"><b><a href="#cite_ref-50">^</a></b></span> <span class="reference-text">Civil law jurisdictions recognise custom as "the other source of law"; hence, scholars tend to divide the civil law into the broad categories of "written law" (<i>ius scriptum</i>) or legislation, and "unwritten law" (<i>ius non scriptum</i>) or custom. Yet they tend to dismiss custom as being of slight importance compared to legislation (Georgiadis, <i>General Principles of Civil Law</i>, 19; Washofsky, <i>Taking Precedent Seriously</i>, 7).</span>
